@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Inventory.Services
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Inventory.ViewModels

@*
    The design's stock table. Rows sort worst-first, so out-of-stock and expired sit at
    the top where they cannot be scrolled past.

    "Use / adjust" is this screen's addition. The design shows only Add to PO and an Edit
    button, but with no scanner there is nothing else that takes stock off the shelf, and a
    count that only ever goes up is a count nobody trusts by Friday. The design's Edit is
    the item name here instead, which is the thing somebody is already looking at.
*@

@*
    Two columns while something is being adjusted, one when nothing is — the list-then-panel
    shape the suppliers, categories, consent-template and role screens all use.

    Conditional rather than always split, unlike those: they list a name and a tag, this is
    an eight-column table. Holding a third of the width in reserve for a panel that is
    usually closed would narrow every row of the common case to pay for the rare one.

    And the 1160px cap lifts while it is open, rather than the panel being carved out of it.
    Both were tried: inside the cap the table came down to 675px and wrapped item names
    across three lines, and a narrower panel instead pushed the segmented control onto two
    rows and split quantity from batch. Eight columns and a form do not fit in 1160 — so the
    panel is added beside the table rather than taken out of it, and the table keeps roughly
    the width it had.
*@
<div class="@(Vm.MovingItem is null
    ? "max-w-[1160px] px-6 py-5"
    : "grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_400px]")">
    <div class="@(Vm.MovingItem is null ? null : "border-divider px-6 py-5 lg:border-r-2")">
    <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <span class="flex-1 text-xs text-ink/55">
            Click an <strong>item's name</strong> to modify or archive it.
            <strong>Use / adjust</strong> records what came off the shelf.
        </span>

        @*
            Batch number is searchable as well as name, category and supplier, because a
            recall arrives as a batch number and "have we still got any of this" is the
            question asked with it in hand.
        *@
        <input class="input w-[220px]" type="search"
               placeholder="Search stock…"
               @bind="Vm.StockSearch" @bind:event="oninput" />

        <button class="btn btn-primary whitespace-nowrap"
                @onclick="() => Vm.NewItemCommand.Execute()">
            New stock item
        </button>
    </div>

    @if (Vm.Stock.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">
            @if (Vm.IsStockNarrowed)
            {
                @* A narrowed list that finds nothing is not an empty cupboard, and the
                   invitation to start adding items would be the wrong answer to it. *@
                <text>
                    Nothing matches. Clear the search to see everything at this location.
                </text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>
                    No stock items at this location yet. Add the consumables, anaesthetics
                    and materials you count, and the reorder list builds itself from the
                    levels you set.
                </text>
            }
        </p>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table class="w-full border-collapse">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th class="head-cell">Item</th>
                        <th class="head-cell">Category</th>
                        <th class="head-cell">On hand</th>
                        <th class="head-cell">Reorder at / qty</th>
                        <th class="head-cell">Batch · expiry</th>
                        <th class="head-cell">Status</th>

                        @*
                            One header per action rather than one for both. The cells below
                            hold a different mix per row — an item already on an order has no
                            Add to PO, one at zero has nothing to use — and a single cell let
                            each row pack its controls from the left, so they landed in a
                            different place on almost every line.

                            Unlabelled: the buttons say what they are, and a heading over a
                            column of identical buttons is a word read once and then in the
                            way.
                        *@
                        <th class="head-cell"></th>
                        <th class="head-cell"></th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    @foreach (var row in Vm.Stock)
                    {
                        @* Tinted while its panel is open, so the row and the panel beside it
                           are visibly the same thing. *@
                        <tr class="@(Vm.IsMoving(row.StockItemId) ? "bg-accent-100" : null)">
                            <td class="body-cell">
                                @*
                                    The name is the way in, in place of an Edit button at the
                                    far end of the row. A column of identical buttons repeated
                                    the one thing every row already says — and on a table this
                                    wide the button was a long way from the name it belonged
                                    to.

                                    A button rather than the whole row being clickable, as the
                                    patients list is: these rows carry their own controls, and
                                    a row that navigates would take somebody to the item
                                    editor when they meant to press Use / adjust.
                                *@
                                <button type="button"
                                        class="cursor-pointer border-0 bg-transparent p-0 text-left font-[inherit] font-bold text-ink hover:text-accent"
                                        @onclick="() => Vm.EditItemCommand.Execute(row.StockItemId)">
                                    @row.Name
                                </button>

                                @if (row.SupplierName is { Length: > 0 } supplier)
                                {
                                    <div class="text-[11px] text-ink/55">@supplier</div>
                                }
                            </td>
                            <td class="body-cell text-xs">@(row.Category ?? "—")</td>
                            <td class="body-cell">
                                @row.OnHand.ToString("0.##")
                                <span class="text-xs text-ink/55">@row.Unit</span>
                            </td>
                            <td class="body-cell text-xs">
                                @row.ReorderLevel.ToString("0.##") /
                                @row.ReorderQuantity.ToString("0.##")
                            </td>
                            <td class="body-cell font-mono text-xs">
                                @if (row.BatchNumber is { Length: > 0 } batch)
                                {
                                    @batch
                                }
                                @if (row.EarliestExpiry is { } expiry)
                                {
                                    <div>@MolargoFormat.Date(expiry)</div>
                                }
                                @if (row.BatchNumber is null && row.EarliestExpiry is null)
                                {
                                    @("—")
                                }
                            </td>
                            <td class="body-cell">
                                <span class="@InventoryCss.StockFlagTag(row.Flag)">
                                    @InventoryCss.StockFlagLabel(row.Flag)
                                </span>
                            </td>
                            @* Ordering. Holds the tag, the button, or nothing. *@
                            <td class="body-cell">
                                @if (row.IsOnOrder)
                                {
                                    @* Already on an open order. Shown instead of the button
                                       so the same item is not ordered twice while the first
                                       delivery is in transit. *@
                                    <span class="tag tag-neutral whitespace-nowrap">
                                        On PO ✓
                                    </span>
                                }
                                else if (row.NeedsOrdering)
                                {
                                    <button class="btn btn-secondary text-xs whitespace-nowrap"
                                            disabled="@Vm.IsBusy"
                                            @onclick="() => Vm.AddToOrderCommand.ExecuteAsync(row.StockItemId)">
                                        Add to PO
                                    </button>
                                }
                            </td>

                            <td class="body-cell">
                                @if (row.OnHand > 0m)
                                {
                                    <button class="btn btn-secondary text-xs whitespace-nowrap"
                                            @onclick="() => Vm.StartMoveCommand.ExecuteAsync(row.StockItemId)">
                                        Use / adjust
                                    </button>
                                }
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        @* The gap from the table above, which this screen owns and the pager cannot know. *@
        <div class="mt-3">
            <PagerComponent Page="Vm.StockPage"
                            PageCount="Vm.StockPageCount"
                            Label="@Vm.StockRangeLabel"
                            OnPrevious="() => Vm.GoToStockPage(Vm.StockPage - 1)"
                            OnNext="() => Vm.GoToStockPage(Vm.StockPage + 1)"
                            OnGoTo="page => Vm.GoToStockPage(page)" />
        </div>

        <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-ink/55">
            @*
                The design promises chairside scanning links the batch to the patient
                automatically. It does not, and the gap matters: batch tracking exists so a
                product recall can find who received which box.
            *@
            On hand is the sum of an item's movements, not a figure anyone types — so the
            count and the history that explains it cannot disagree. Batch and expiry are
            recorded when stock is received against a purchase order.
            <strong>No barcode scanning is built</strong>, so a batch is not yet linked to
            the patient it was used on; that link needs a scanner at the chair.
        </p>
    }
    </div>

    @*
        The adjustment panel, on the right where every other editor in the app sits. It was
        inside the row's last cell, which made a wide table grow a form in one of its cells —
        the table reflowed as it opened, and the fields landed wherever the column happened
        to be wide enough.
    *@
    @if (Vm.MovingItem is { } moving)
    {
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 px-6 py-5">
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-2.5">
                <h5 class="m-0">@moving.Name</h5>

                <span class="text-xs text-ink/55">
                    @moving.OnHand.ToString("0.##") @moving.Unit on hand
                </span>
            </div>

            <div>
                <span class="label-field">What happened to it</span>

                <div class="seg mt-1">
                    @foreach (var kind in InventoryViewModel.MoveKinds)
                    {
                        <button type="button" class="@KindCss(kind)"
                                @onclick="() => Vm.SetMoveKindCommand.Execute(kind)">
                            @InventoryViewModel.KindLabel(kind)
                        </button>
                    }
                </div>
            </div>

            @*
                Quantity and batch on one line. They are one question — how many, out of
                which box — and stacking them made the panel taller for no gain.
            *@
            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-end gap-2">
                <div class="field w-[110px]">
                    <label class="label-field" for="move-quantity">
                        Quantity@(moving.Unit is { Length: > 0 } unit ? $" ({unit})" : null)
                    </label>
                    <input id="move-quantity" class="input" inputmode="decimal"
                           @bind="Vm.MoveQuantity" @bind:event="oninput" />
                </div>

                @if (moving.BatchNumber is { Length: > 0 } onShelf)
                {
                    <div class="field w-[190px]">
                        <label class="label-field" for="move-batch">Batch</label>

                        @* Placeholder rather than a value: the batch on the shelf is what
                           this came from unless somebody says otherwise, and prefilling the
                           box would record that batch even where a different one was
                           opened. *@
                        <input id="move-batch" class="input" placeholder="@onShelf"
                               @bind="Vm.MoveBatch" @bind:event="oninput" />
                    </div>
                }
            </div>

            @if (moving.BatchNumber is { Length: > 0 } shelf)
            {
                <div class="text-[11px] text-ink/55">
                    Leave the batch to use @shelf, the one expiring first.
                </div>
            }

            @if (Vm.MoveKind == StockMovementKind.Adjustment)
            {
                <div class="bg-surface p-2.5 text-xs text-ink/70">
                    @*
                        Said before the number is typed. An adjustment here reduces the
                        count — it is a stocktake finding less on the shelf than the ledger
                        says, not a way to type in a new total.
                    *@
                    <strong>This records a shortfall.</strong>
                    Enter how many are missing, not what the new total should be — on hand
                    is the sum of the movements, and typing a total here would break that.
                </div>
            }

            <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 border-t-2 border-divider pt-3">
                <button class="btn btn-primary whitespace-nowrap"
                        disabled="@(!Vm.CanApplyMove)"
                        @onclick="() => Vm.ApplyMoveCommand.ExecuteAsync()">
                    Record
                </button>

                <button class="btn btn-ghost text-xs"
                        @onclick="() => Vm.CancelMoveCommand.Execute()">
                    Cancel
                </button>
            </div>

            @*
                The item's own ledger, under the form. On hand is the sum of these, so this
                is the working that produces the number at the top of the panel — and it
                answers the question somebody actually has at the cupboard, which is whether
                they already recorded this.
            *@
            <div class="border-t-2 border-divider pt-3">
                <h6 class="mt-0 mb-2">Recent movements</h6>

                @if (Vm.Movements.Count == 0)
                {
                    <p class="m-0 text-sm text-ink/55">
                        Nothing recorded against this item yet. On hand came from the
                        opening count when it was added.
                    </p>
                }
                else
                {
                    <div class="flex flex-col">
                        @foreach (var movement in Vm.Movements)
                        {
                            <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 border-b border-divider py-1.5 last:border-b-0">
                                <span class="w-[86px] shrink-0 text-[11px] text-ink/55">
                                    @MolargoFormat.DayLabel(movement.OccurredUtc)
                                </span>

                                <span class="min-w-0 flex-1 text-xs">
                                    @InventoryViewModel.KindLabel(movement.Kind)

                                    @if (movement.BatchNumber is { Length: > 0 } batch)
                                    {
                                        <span class="font-mono text-[11px] text-ink/55">
                                            · @batch
                                        </span>
                                    }
                                </span>

                                @* Signed, so a receipt and a use are told apart at a glance
                                   rather than by reading the kind. *@
                                <span class="w-[52px] shrink-0 text-right text-xs @(movement.QuantityChange < 0m
                                    ? "text-accent-800" : null)">
                                    @movement.QuantityChange.ToString("+0.##;-0.##;0")
                                </span>

                                <span class="w-[42px] shrink-0 text-right text-xs text-ink/55">
                                    @movement.BalanceAfter.ToString("0.##")
                                </span>
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>

                    <p class="mt-2 mb-0 text-[11px] text-ink/55">
                        @*
                            The cap is stated rather than left to be discovered. The service
                            takes the most recent thirty, and a list that stops without
                            saying so reads as a complete history — which for a batch recall
                            is the one thing it must not be mistaken for.
                        *@
                        @(Vm.Movements.Count < 30
                            ? "The full history for this item."
                            : "The most recent 30. Older movements are on the item's own page.")
                    </p>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public InventoryViewModel Vm { get; set; } = default!;

    private string KindCss(StockMovementKind kind) =>
        Vm.MoveKind == kind ? "seg-opt seg-opt-on" : "seg-opt";
}
